# SCM syntax highlighting & preventing 3-way merges
pixi.lock merge=binary linguist-language=YAML linguist-generated=true -diff

# gofmt is byte-strict about LF-only line endings (M21 Phase 5, #242 — learned on the since-deleted
# Go sidecar); kept generic so any future Go file checks out LF on Windows.
*.go text eol=lf

# Shell scripts and patch files must stay LF on every platform. Windows-only (#1405), but this is
# still load-bearing: scripts/verify-pack-roundtrip.sh and .githooks/pre-push both run through a
# POSIX-shell interpreter (Git Bash) on the operator's own Windows machine, and a CRLF `#!/bin/sh`
# first line breaks Git Bash identically to how it broke a Linux CI clone -- there is no CI job left
# to protect, but there is still a local Git Bash execution to protect. `git apply` also rejects a
# CRLF patch against LF sources.
*.sh text eol=lf
*.patch -text

# Git hooks are shell scripts with no extension, so `*.sh` above does not reach them. Same reasoning
# as above, one step earlier: a CRLF `#!/bin/sh` is not a valid shebang, so the hook would not merely
# misbehave under the operator's own Git Bash -- it would fail to execute at all, and a hook that
# cannot run is a gate that silently is not there.
.githooks/* text eol=lf

# The committed vendor-survey index (#589). Generated on whichever machine ran the survey and read
# by `audit-completeness` in CI (windows-latest, #1405) -- pin LF so the file does not churn from
# Windows `core.autocrlf` on a tracked generated file.
.vendor-survey/ledger.tsv text eol=lf

# Test fixtures must preserve LF line endings across platforms (#1673)
tests/fixtures/* text eol=lf

# Derived benchmark tables (#1867): `derive_scores.py --check` compares the committed file byte-for-byte
# against a fresh LF derivation, so a CRLF checkout on Windows would read as stale on a correct file.
benchmarks/deepswe/*/derived-scores.csv text eol=lf

# Committed cost-ledger exports and their derivation (#1901 C3). Same reason one line up, twice over:
# `baton ledger export` writes LF explicitly (LedgerCsv's own promise, so the same query compares
# byte-equal across machines) and `derive.py --check` compares its committed outputs byte-for-byte
# against a fresh LF derivation.
benchmarks/ledger/*.csv text eol=lf
benchmarks/ledger/fixtures/*.csv text eol=lf
benchmarks/ledger/medians.json text eol=lf
benchmarks/ledger/medians.md text eol=lf

